Step-by-Step 
Fix1. 
Check for Existing Keys
Open your terminal and run:
ls -al ~/.ssh
Look for files named id_ed25519.pub or id_rsa.pub. 
If you see one, skip to Step 3.

If u dont see do step2
Step2. 
First check ur configured email id using below command in terminal
git config --global user.email
Save this

Next
Generate a New SSH Key
If no keys exist, run this command (replace with your GitHub email):
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com"
Press Enter to accept the default file location.
Enter a secure passphrase (optional, press Enter to skip).

3. Add Key to SSH Agent
Start the background agent and register your key:
Mac/Linux: eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
Mac Only: ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Windows/Linux: ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed255194. 

Link Key to GitHub
Copy the public key text to your clipboard:
Mac: pbcopy < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
Windows (Git Bash): cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub | clipLinux: cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub (then manually copy the output)

Go to GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG keys → New SSH Key. 
Paste the text and save.

5. Test connection
Run in terminal
ssh -T git@github.com
Type yes if prompted about host authenticity.

---
1. Add Key to Your Local SSH Agent
Run the following commands to ensure your operating system uses the key automatically:
For Mac / Linux:eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 (If you on Mac, use ssh-add --apple-use-keychain ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 instead)
For Windows (Git Bash):eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

2. Copy the Public Key Text
You need to copy the contents of the public file (.pub). 
Run the command for your system to copy it instantly to your clipboard:
Windows (Git Bash): cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub | clipMac: pbcopy < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
Linux: cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub (Select the printed text from the terminal and copy it)

3. Add to GitHub Settings
Open your browser and go to your GitHub SSH Keys Settings.
Click the green New SSH Key button in the top right.
Give it a Title (like "My Laptop").
Keep the Key type as Authentication Key.
Paste your copied text into the Key box.
Click Add SSH Key.

4. Test the Connection
Verify it works by running this command in your terminal:
ssh -T git@github.com

If prompted with a warning about "authenticity of host", type yes and press Enter. 
You should see a success message containing your username